Concern 2 is the one that breaks the case. The product exists to inform a decision about closure, and that is exactly where our evidence is thinnest. Closure rests on testing, release approvals and attestations that sit outside these tables. The claim boundary I wrote (“the recorded remediation does not clearly align with the recorded weakness”) is honest, but it is weak. A reviewer will reasonably ask what they are getting beyond “go and look at the evidence”. That is also the likely Line 1 response (Concern 3): “the closure was approved, and the evidence is elsewhere”. Framing the output as a request for evidence rather than a challenge helps at the margin. It does not change the fact that the product’s value is bounded by how much review capacity exists and how often a partial-view flag is right.

Concern 4 is also right. My first answer said incidents are the only dataset that records what happened. The current design uses them for one retrieval step. The closure-review product mostly analyses documents about documents.

Concern 5, one correction. I earlier treated product as unstructured. The incident schema has INC_PRD_LVL_1 and INC_PRD_LVL_2, INC_PRCSS, and INC_OTHR_SYS. If those fields are populated and resolvable (unverified, per profile checks P8 and P9), the operating-model context for an incident sits on the incident itself. That removes the dependency on Cross Ref for the outcome side and makes concentration analysis much simpler than I assumed.

What the one slide should say

Roughly: “[X]% of loss and customer harm over [N] years comes from [M] process, application and product combinations, and [K] failure mechanisms recur across [J] business units. [Y]% of those combinations have had no issue or action activity in the last [Z] years, while [W]% of remediation effort goes to areas accounting for [V]% of harm.” That is a format with placeholders, not a finding.

The decision it supports is where to direct remediation funding and assurance effort next planning cycle, and which cross-cutting mechanisms deserve an enterprise-level owner. That is a decision a CRO and executive committee actually own.

