The replacement: incident-led concentration and attention study

Unit of analysis: the incident, grouped into operating-model cells (process, application, product) and failure mechanisms. This returns to the original logic.

Three outputs, in order:

Concentration: where consequence sits, reported in the three lanes from earlier (material and recurring, severe and novel, frequent and low impact), never summed.
Cross-cutting mechanisms: the same mechanism appearing across several cells or business units under different labels. This is where text reasoning adds something structured fields cannot.
Attention gap: how issue and action activity (via Cross Ref) lines up against consequence by cell. Both directions matter: high consequence with little attention, and heavy attention on low consequence.

Controls stay out of the first study. They can become a coverage lens once the cells are validated.

Why this is simpler than the current MVP: it needs one text-reasoning task (mechanism tagging on incidents), no temporal anchoring, no action-set mapping, no review queue, and no history reconstruction. It is a time-bound study, not a platform. It also challenges no one’s decision, which sidesteps the adoption problem. Findings are about operating arrangements, not about whether a closure was sound.

A cheap kill test first (about two weeks, structured fields only):

Concentration of write-offs, customer impact and severity by process and by product, with no text work.
Cross Ref issue and action counts against the same cells.
Question for the head of risk: would any of this surprise them?

If concentration is already well known and attention already tracks it, the insight is thin and I would stop, or narrow to the cross-cutting mechanisms alone.

Risks I would hold to:

Chance concentrations. Thousands of cells will produce many apparent hotspots by chance. Find concentrations on data up to year T and test whether they persist afterwards. Report only the ones that do.
Reporting bias. Concentration partly reflects where people log incidents. Use the reporting indicators already specified (logging lag, low-impact share, near-miss share) as caveats on every cell.
Heavy tails. A few events will dominate loss. Keep the lanes separate and show the severe tail on its own.
Write-offs understate cost, and process fields may be free text. Both are profile questions, not assumptions.
Cell size. Minimum cell counts, and no owner or team views, as before.
Claims. Associations only. “Attention” is activity recorded, not effectiveness.
Would I fund the current MVP as the one capability?

No. If the incident-led study works, it produces what the triage product could not: an enterprise view of where the operating model fails and whether effort follows. Closure triage then becomes a possible follow-on, targeted at cells the study shows are under-served, and by then the closure-evidence question can be asked of specific cases rather than a whole population.

What carries over from the work so far:

Profiling checks P0 (snapshots), P4 (Cross Ref reconciliation), P5 (text quality and leakage), P8 (incident pools and reporting indicators), P9 (entity resolution), P10 (eras), P11 (privacy), P12 (tokens).
The evidence classes, the no-causal-claims rules, the lane structure and the no-people-ranking guardrails.
The 1A mapping, extended for incident fields.

What I would shelve: LLAC and the temporal period model, action-set mapping, the eligibility routes, the review queue and the reviewer outcome codes.

One caution on my own recommendation

The closure-review product had a clear owner and checkable ground truth. The incident-led study has a clearer value story but a softer owner. Before committing, name the executive who would take the slide to the planning cycle and say what they would change. If no one will, I would fall back to the narrow cross-cutting mechanisms piece.

Next step, if the team agrees: I would replace the pilot scorecard with a two-week specification for the kill test above (fields, cells, lanes, the Cross Ref attention measure and the “would this surprise you” review), and update the profiling checks for the incident-first view.
